  "markscheme": {
    "html": "<p>Correct answer: C</p>\n<p>A: Incorrect. This corresponds to incorrectly using Celsius in the proportionality: <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T_2=1.5\\times 27^\\circ\\text{C}=40.5^\\circ\\text{C}\"></span>, then converting to kelvin: <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"40.5+273=313.5\\text{ K}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>B: Incorrect. This does not follow <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{P}{T}=\\text{constant}\"></span> for a fixed mass at constant volume.</p>\n<p>C: Correct. At constant volume, <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\frac{P}{T}=\\text{constant}\"></span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T_2=T_1\\frac{P_2}{P_1}=(27+273)\\times\\frac{150}{100}=300\\times 1.5=450\\text{ K}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>D: Incorrect. This is the result of converting <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"450^\\circ\\text{C}\"></span> to kelvin (i.e. adding 273 to the wrong Celsius value) rather than calculating <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T_2\"></span> in kelvin.</p>"
  },
  "paper": "paper_1a",
  "question": {
    "html": "<p>A fixed mass of an ideal gas is contained in a rigid tank at a temperature of <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"27^\\circ\\text{C}\"></span>. If the pressure of the gas increases from <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"100\\text{ kPa}\"></span> to <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"150\\text{ kPa}\"></span>, what is the final temperature of the gas in kelvin?</p><ol class=\"mcq-options\"><li data-label=\"A\"><p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"313.5\\text{ K}\"></span></p></li><li data-label=\"B\"><p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"423\\text{ K}\"></span></p></li><li data-label=\"C\"><p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"450\\text{ K}\"></span></p></li><li data-label=\"D\"><p><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"723\\text{ K}\"></span></p></li></ol>"
  },
